\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two onsite masses on the two-by-two torus}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use the sign-line exchange of \ref{fdip-011O} on the
cellular #{2\times2} torus of \ref{fdip-00U9}, with every
positive labeled link the identity and the scalar constantly
#{1}. The two onsite eigenlines are now preserved by every link.
For each of their masses #{a=m+y} and #{a=m-y}, the kernel is
#{a\bbI+\extD_1} on scalar cochains.}

\p{The #{4} site characters have translation signs
#{(1,1),(-1,1),(1,-1),(-1,-1)}. They give a basis because their
#{4} sign patterns on the sites are pairwise orthogonal and
nonzero. On a character with signs #{(\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2)},
the shift and its inverse have the same sign in each direction.
The cochain term on the #{4} exterior slots is therefore
##{\sum_{i=1}^2(\epsilon_i-1)
              \big((e_i\wedge)+(e_i\cdot)\big).}
Wedge plus contraction squares to the identity for each unit
direction, and distinct directions anticommute by \ref{fdip-00QA}.
Hence the square is scalar with the respective values
#{0,4,4,8}. For the first character the cochain term is zero.
For each other character let #{b>0} be the square root of its
squared value. The real self-adjoint cochain term has eigenvalues
in #{\{b,-b\}}. Exterior parity anticommutes with it and
interchanges the two eigenspaces, so each has dimension two.}

\p{For a single mass the constant character contributes #{a^4};
the other characters contribute #{(a^2-4)^2}, #{(a^2-4)^2}
and #{(a^2-8)^2}. Put #{a_\sigma=m+\sigma y}. Multiplying over
the two fibre lines gives
##{\det K_{1,\phi^\circ}=
 \prod_{\sigma=\pm1}a_\sigma^4
 (a_\sigma^2-4)^4(a_\sigma^2-8)^2.}
The product counts both lines when #{y=0} and holds also when
one of the factors vanishes.}
